<Project>
  <!--
    Central pins for transitive NuGet dependencies that NuGet's audit flags.

    Directory.Build.props sets TreatWarningsAsErrors, so NuGet audit findings
    (NU1902/NU1903/NU1904) are build errors. That is deliberate, but the advisory can
    belong to a package no project references directly, and then no project file has
    anywhere obvious to fix it:

      MimeKit 4.18.0             -> System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs 10.0.0
      fpssoftware.chassis 1.3.0  -> System.Security.Cryptography.Xml  10.0.15

    10.0.0 through 10.0.12 are all covered by the advisories below. 10.0.13 is the first
    release outside every affected range, so it is the floor - not "the latest". Both pins
    sit at 10.0.15 because chassis 1.3.0 asks for that Xml version and Xml 10.0.15 itself
    requires Pkcs >= 10.0.15; pinning either lower makes NuGet report a package downgrade
    (NU1605), which TreatWarningsAsErrors turns into a build error.

      GHSA-846f-f764-76mx  high      Pkcs  10.0.0 ..= 10.0.12
      GHSA-2fmv-rv2c-59x7  moderate  Pkcs  10.0.0 ..= 10.0.12
      GHSA-7hqp-7c5m-4vq6  high      Xml   10.0.0 ..= 10.0.12
      GHSA-c399-9853-6jmj  high      Xml   10.0.0 ..= 10.0.12
      GHSA-r955-8q5j-3h83  high      Xml   10.0.0 ..= 10.0.12

    Include, not Update: an `Update` transform only runs against an item that already
    exists, and a transitive package has no PackageReference item of its own, so `Update`
    here would be a silent no-op and the advisory would still fail the build.

    This lives in Directory.Build.targets rather than in the consuming projects because
    Project Reference restore writes each project its own project.assets.json: a pin
    declared once here reaches every project, whereas a per-project override only fixes
    the project that declares it and leaves its referenced projects failing.

    Raise these when a newer patched release ships. An entry can be deleted once the
    packages that request it start asking for a fixed version on their own, but the Xml pin
    is kept deliberately at 10.0.15 even though chassis 1.3.0 now does: the guard script
    requires the pin, and it stops a future requester from resolving below the floor. For a
    new transitively flagged package, pin it the same way and add its id and floor to
    scripts/nuget-vulnerability-pinning.test.ps1, which enforces these floors in CI.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs" Version="10.0.15" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Xml" Version="10.0.15" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
